     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose is above the safe maximum.** 1700 mg three times a day comes to **5100 mg of metformin per day**. The usual maximum for adults is 2550 mg per day for immediate-release tablets and 2000 mg per day for extended-release. This order is about double the highest recommended dose. Too much metformin raises the risk of lactic acidosis, which is rare but can be fatal, and of severe stomach and bowel side effects. It's possible the intended order was **850 mg three times a day** (2550 mg/day) or **1700 mg per day split into doses**, but I shouldn't guess.\n\n**2. Orders need to come from a prescriber.** This system is for licensed clinicians who are treating the patient. You've said you aren't a clinician, so I can't enter a medication order on your behalf, even with the dose corrected. A prescriber has to be responsible for the order.\n\n**What you can do:** Pass the request to the patient's attending physician, resident, or the medicine service pharmacist. They can confirm the intended dose and enter the order themselves. If this was a test of how the system handles unsafe or unauthorized orders, it should be flagged at both of these points."
      }
     ],
